from docxlib import h1, h2, h3, p, li, caption, table, note

BLOCKS = [
h1("CHAPTER 1: INTRODUCTION"),
h2("1.1 Background of the Study"),
p("Over the past fifteen years the way organisations build computing infrastructure has changed completely. Servers that were once ordered, racked and configured by hand are now created by an application programming interface call and destroyed minutes later. Amazon Web Services (AWS), Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud have made this the normal way to work, and with it has come a new engineering practice: Infrastructure as Code (IaC). Instead of clicking through a web console to create a network or a database, an engineer writes a file that describes what the infrastructure should look like, keeps that file in version control, and lets a tool make reality match the file (Rahman, Mahdavi-Hezaveh, & Williams, 2019)."),
p("Terraform, released by HashiCorp in 2014, has become one of the most widely used tools for this work. It uses a declarative language, so the engineer states the desired end state rather than the steps to reach it, and it shows a plan of what it intends to change before it changes anything. Recent experimental work found that Terraform provisioned an AWS environment in roughly 253 seconds where the AWS-native alternative needed roughly 623 seconds, and attributed Terraform's lower failure rate to its syntax validation, plan previews and state management (Regvart et al., 2026). Interview evidence confirms its position in practice: in a study of 22 experienced practitioners, GitHub Actions had become the dominant continuous integration tool, and Terraform appeared alongside it among the tools that teams automate (Rostami Mazrae et al., 2023)."),
p("At the same time, the delivery side of software has been automated thoroughly. Continuous integration and continuous delivery (CI/CD) pipelines now compile code, run tests, scan for vulnerabilities and publish artefacts without a human touching them. GitHub Actions, launched in November 2019, became the most used CI service on GitHub within eighteen months, mainly because it is built into the platform where the code already lives (Decan et al., 2022; Rostami Mazrae et al., 2023). Earlier work established what this automation is worth: across 34,544 open-source projects, those using continuous integration released more than twice as often as those that did not (Hilton et al., 2016)."),
p("These two developments have not always arrived together. Many organisations automate the application first, because that is where the daily pressure is felt, and leave the infrastructure until later. The result is a pipeline that is automated at one end and manual at the other. Application code passes through build, test and security scanning on its way to a container registry, while the network, load balancer, container service, gateway and access policies that receive it were created by hand and are changed by hand. Nothing records who changed what, nothing keeps the development, staging and production environments the same, and nothing except an engineer's memory can rebuild an environment from scratch."),
p("This gap matters more in some sectors than others. Cambodia's banking sector has grown quickly, and financial institutions operate under supervision from the National Bank of Cambodia, which expects controlled and auditable change management in core systems. In that setting an infrastructure change that leaves no reviewable record is not simply untidy; it is a control weakness. Yet the published evidence on IaC comes almost entirely from open-source repositories and from research groups in Europe and North America. No study known to the researcher has examined IaC adoption in a Cambodian organisation, which means local engineering teams making this decision have had to rely on evidence gathered elsewhere."),
p("Against this background, the present study took one documented, containerised AWS architecture, wrote it as Terraform modules, ran those modules through a GitHub Actions pipeline, and measured what changed. It combined controlled, repeated measurement, which establishes how large and how stable the effects are, with an analysis of the implementation record, which explains what made the change difficult. The aim was neither to advocate for Terraform nor to dismiss manual work, but to produce evidence that an engineering team in this setting could use."),
h2("1.2 Statement of the Research Problem"),
p("The architecture examined in this study is a set of five containerised microservices (authentication, messaging, user profile, finance and file sharing) delivered by a pipeline that handles application code well. A commit to the application repository triggers an automated build, and the resulting container image is pushed to Amazon Elastic Container Registry (ECR), where it is scanned for vulnerabilities. Before this study the automation stopped at that point. It covered the application, not the infrastructure the application runs on."),
p("The AWS environment itself was not defined as code. The Virtual Private Cloud (VPC), its subnets in two Availability Zones, the Internet and Network Address Translation (NAT) gateways, route tables, security groups, the Application Load Balancer (ALB), the Elastic Container Service (ECS) clusters, task definitions and Fargate services, the API Gateway and its private VPC Link, the Identity and Access Management (IAM) roles and policies, the monitoring alarms and the scaling policies were all created and modified through the console or the command line by hand."),
p("Three consequences follow. First, infrastructure changes were not version-controlled and not peer-reviewed, so there was no record of who changed what, when, or why. In a regulated financial institution this is a compliance problem as well as an engineering one. Second, nothing kept development, staging and production aligned, so environments could drift apart, making testing in a lower environment progressively less trustworthy. Third, rebuilding an environment depended on what individual engineers remembered rather than on code that could simply be run again, which matters for disaster recovery, for expanding into another region, and for creating an isolated environment in which to test."),
p("What is contested is not whether this is a problem but how much difference fixing it makes. One position holds that the benefit is obvious and large, and points to frequently repeated claims of deployment-time reductions of around 50 per cent. A second position holds that the benefit is offset by new costs: state file management, secrets handling, a steep learning curve, and the ongoing maintenance of modules that go out of date. There is evidence for the second position. A study of Terraform module dependencies found that teams postpone updates and that the resulting technical lag can persist for months (Begoug, Ouni, & Chouchen, 2025), and a study of 812 open-source Terraform projects found that developers apply access-control policies fairly consistently but neglect encryption at rest (Verdet et al., 2025). Writing infrastructure as code does not by itself make it well configured."),
p("Deciding between these positions requires measurement in a specific setting, and that measurement did not exist. The problem this study addresses is therefore the absence of a designed, implemented and measured Terraform codebase, integrated into a GitHub Actions pipeline, for a defined containerised AWS architecture, evaluated against the manual provisioning it is intended to replace."),
h2("1.3 Research Gap"),
p("Three gaps follow from the discussion above and are developed more fully in Chapter 2."),
p("The empirical gap is that no published study located in this review measures Terraform against manual provisioning of the same architecture. The closest available work compares Terraform against other IaC tools, namely Pulumi and AWS CloudFormation, on an architecture similar to the one studied here (Regvart et al., 2026). That design answers the question of which tool to choose. It does not answer the question facing an organisation that has no IaC at all, which is whether adopting any of them is worth the effort."),
p("The theoretical gap concerns pipeline integration. Existing work treats IaC and CI/CD as separate objects of study. Terraform research examines code, modules and security in isolation from any pipeline (Begoug, Ouni, & Chouchen, 2025; Verdet et al., 2025), while CI/CD research examines pipelines without asking what they provision (Hilton et al., 2016; Kinsman et al., 2021). Yet the control benefit of IaC, meaning review, approval and an audit trail, only arrives when infrastructure changes are forced through the pipeline. Running Terraform from a laptop produces a version-controlled file but no reviewed change. This study therefore treats pipeline integration depth as a distinct construct rather than an implementation detail."),
p("The contextual gap is the absence of evidence from Cambodia. The reviewed literature draws on open-source repositories and on studies conducted in Europe and North America. Findings generated in those settings cannot simply be assumed to transfer to an organisation operating under different regulatory expectations, with different team sizes and different levels of prior tooling experience."),
p("This study addressed all three gaps together. It compared IaC with manual provisioning rather than with a rival tool; it modelled pipeline integration depth explicitly; and it did so in a Cambodian organisational setting for which no comparable evidence exists."),
h2("1.4 Research Aim"),
p("The aim of this study was to design, implement and evaluate a modular Terraform codebase for a containerised AWS architecture, integrated into a GitHub Actions CI/CD pipeline, in order to establish how far this approach improves provisioning duration, environment consistency, reproducibility and version-control coverage compared with manual provisioning, and to explain the practical barriers encountered in making the change."),
h2("1.5 Research Objectives"),
p("The study pursued the following specific objectives:"),
li("1. To document how the target architecture was provisioned and to identify which components were created by hand, absent from version control, or configured differently across environments."),
li("2. To design and build a modular Terraform codebase that provisions the full architecture, using an Amazon Simple Storage Service (S3) backend for remote state and Amazon DynamoDB for state locking, parameterised so that the same code can serve more than one environment."),
li("3. To integrate Terraform into a GitHub Actions pipeline that can destroy, rebuild and redeploy the environment and record the timing of every stage."),
li("4. To measure provisioning duration, configuration drift, reproducibility and version-control coverage under the Terraform approach and compare them with manual provisioning."),
li("5. To identify the practical barriers and enablers encountered during implementation, and to propose evidence-based recommendations for engineering teams, platform managers and technology governance functions in Cambodia."),
h2("1.6 Research Questions and Hypotheses"),
h3("1.6.1 Research Questions"),
p("The study was guided by one overarching question: what difference does a Terraform-based, pipeline-integrated approach make to the provisioning of a containerised AWS architecture, and what makes that change difficult in practice? This was addressed through four subsidiary questions:"),
li("RQ1. How is the target architecture provisioned, and what proportion of its components are under version control?"),
li("RQ2. To what extent does Terraform-based provisioning differ from manual provisioning in provisioning duration, configuration drift, reproducibility and version-control coverage?"),
li("RQ3. Does the depth of pipeline integration moderate the relationship between provisioning method and configuration drift?"),
li("RQ4. What practical barriers and enablers arise when an AWS architecture is moved onto Terraform and integrated into a CI/CD pipeline?"),
p("RQ1 to RQ3 were addressed in the quantitative strand; RQ4 was addressed in the qualitative strand and was also used to interpret the results of RQ2 and RQ3. The alignment between questions, data sources and analytical techniques is set out in Table 3.1."),
h3("1.6.2 Hypotheses"),
p("Consistent with the framework developed in Section 2.9, the following directional hypotheses were proposed for the quantitative strand:"),
li("H1. Terraform-based provisioning produces a significantly shorter provisioning duration than manual provisioning."),
li("H2. Terraform-based provisioning produces significantly lower configuration drift between environments than manual provisioning."),
li("H3. Terraform-based provisioning achieves a significantly higher rate of successful destroy-and-rebuild reproduction than manual provisioning."),
li("H4. Integrating Terraform into the GitHub Actions pipeline significantly increases the proportion of infrastructure components that are under version control and subject to peer review."),
li("H5. Pipeline integration depth significantly moderates the relationship between provisioning method and configuration drift, such that the reduction in drift is larger when changes must pass a gated plan and apply than when Terraform is run locally without review."),
p("Each hypothesis was stated in the alternative form and evaluated at the conventional significance level of _p_ < .05, with effect sizes reported alongside significance values so that practical as well as statistical significance could be judged (Cohen, 1988; Field, 2018). Chapter 4 reports which hypotheses the collected data could test and which they could not."),
h2("1.7 Significance of the Study"),
p("Theoretically, the study contributes to the IaC literature by changing the comparison. Existing experimental work asks which IaC tool performs best; this study asks what IaC is worth compared with no IaC at all, which is the decision an organisation without it actually faces. It also treats pipeline integration depth as a construct in its own right, on the argument that the governance benefits of IaC are produced by the review gate rather than by the file format."),
p("Practically, the study gives engineering teams and platform managers a module structure and pipeline configuration they can reuse, together with measured figures for provisioning duration, destruction duration, service recovery time and reproducibility. Because engineering time is finite, evidence about the size of the benefit and the cost of obtaining it is directly usable in planning. The qualitative strand adds the implementation detail that measurement alone cannot supply, indicating not only what improves but what is likely to go wrong on the way."),
p("At the governance level, the study speaks to a control requirement rather than a preference. Where infrastructure changes must be auditable, the version-control coverage measure is a compliance indicator, not a convenience. Supplying Cambodian evidence on this point responds to a gap that imported studies cannot fill."),
p("Methodologically, the study shows how an explanatory sequential mixed methods design can be applied to an engineering artefact rather than to a human population, using controlled repeated deployment for the quantitative strand and the version-control history of the implementation for the qualitative strand. For the University of Puthisastra, it contributes a documented, reusable measurement procedure and Terraform codebase that later student projects can extend."),
h2("1.8 Scope and Delimitations"),
p("The study was delimited in four respects. In terms of content, it covered only the components of the architecture described in Section 1.2 and the four outcome measures specified in the conceptual framework. Other plausible outcomes of IaC adoption, such as monthly cloud cost or team onboarding time, are acknowledged but were not measured. In terms of tooling, it examined Terraform only; Pulumi and AWS CloudFormation are discussed in Chapter 2 as context but were not tested, since Regvart et al. (2026) have already compared them."),
p("In terms of environment, all provisioning runs were performed in an isolated AWS account in a single region. No change was made to any live environment of the case organisation, and no production or customer data was accessed. Temporally, module development took place in December 2025 and the measurement campaign on 14 and 15 October 2026; because Terraform and GitHub Actions change frequently, results apply to the versions recorded in Chapter 4."),
p("These delimitations were deliberate choices made to keep the study achievable within the time available for a master's dissertation. Their implications for the interpretation of results are revisited in Section 3.12 and discussed again in Chapters 5 and 6."),
h2("1.9 Definition of Key Terms"),
p("The following terms are used throughout this dissertation with the meanings given below. Conceptual definitions state what each construct means theoretically; operational definitions state how it was measured in this study."),
caption("Table 1.1", "Definition of Key Terms"),
table(["Term", "Conceptual Definition", "Operational Definition in This Study"], [
    ["Infrastructure as Code", "Defining and managing computing infrastructure through machine-readable definition files kept under version control (Rahman, Mahdavi-Hezaveh, & Williams, 2019; Kumara et al., 2021).", "Provisioning performed by executing the Terraform configuration stored in the project repository."],
    ["Provisioning method", "The means by which cloud resources are created, changed and destroyed.", "Two levels: Terraform executed by the GitHub Actions pipeline, and manual creation through the AWS console (simulated in this study)."],
    ["Provisioning duration", "Elapsed wall-clock time needed to bring an environment from an empty state to a fully working state.", "Seconds from the start of the first create step to all five ECS services reaching steady state, recorded by the pipeline per phase."],
    ["Configuration drift", "Unintended divergence in configuration between environments meant to be equivalent (Kumara et al., 2021).", "Count of resource attributes that differ between environments, excluding a pre-declared list of intentional differences."],
    ["Reproducibility", "The ability to recreate an environment identically from its definition.", "Proportion of destroy-and-rebuild cycles that completed without error and returned every service to a healthy steady state."],
    ["Version-control coverage", "The extent to which infrastructure components are defined in files held in version control.", "Components of the architecture inventory fully defined in the repository, divided by all components, as a percentage."],
    ["Pipeline integration depth", "The degree to which infrastructure changes are compelled to pass through automated validation and human review before taking effect.", "Ordered levels: (1) local execution, no review; (2) pipeline execution; (3) plan on pull request with gated apply on merge."],
    ["Technical lag", "The period during which a configuration remains on an older version than the latest upstream release (Begoug, Ouni, & Chouchen, 2025).", "Not measured directly; used to justify version pinning."],
], [1900, 3370, 3370]),
note("Every construct shown in the conceptual framework in Figure 2.1 appears in this table."),
h2("1.10 Organisation of the Dissertation"),
p("This dissertation is presented in six chapters. Chapter 1 has introduced the research by setting out its background, problem, gap, aim, objectives, questions, hypotheses, significance and scope. Chapter 2 reviews the theoretical and empirical literature, summarises and compares twenty prior studies, establishes the research gap and derives the conceptual framework and hypotheses. Chapter 3 explains the research philosophy, design, sampling, instruments, procedures, analytical techniques, quality criteria and ethical safeguards. Chapter 4 presents the quantitative and qualitative results and their integration, without interpretation. Chapter 5 discusses those results in relation to the literature and proposes a revised conceptual framework. Chapter 6 states the conclusions reached against each objective, the contributions, recommendations and limitations of the study, and directions for future research."),
]
